.SH "NAME"
clevercsv-segments \- Detect contiguous dialect segments in a CSV file
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
.sp
.nf
\fIclevercsv segments [\-e ENCODING | \-\-encoding=ENCODING] [\-d DELIMITERS |
                    \-\-delimiters=DELIMITERS] [\-\-min\-support=MIN_SUPPORT]
                    [\-\-tolerance=TOLERANCE] [\-j | \-\-json] <path>
.fi
.sp
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
.sp
Detect contiguous segments with different dialects in a CSV file. This is useful when a file concatenates exports from different systems, for example a first part delimited by commas followed by a part delimited by semicolons. The command reports the record and character offsets of each segment together with the dialect detected for each segment, so that each part can be read separately. Boundaries are only reported between complete records; records with embedded newlines are never split. When no reliable boundary can be established, the command exits with status 3 and leaves the file for manual review; the regular 'detect' command remains available for single\-dialect files.
.SH "OPTIONS"
.sp
.sp
.sp
\-h, \-\-help
.RS 4
show this help message and exit
.RE
.PP
\-e, \-\-encoding
.RS 4
The file encoding of the given CSV file is automatically detected using chardet. While chardet is incredibly accurate, it is not perfect. In the rare cases that it makes a mistake in detecting the file encoding, you can override the encoding by providing it through this flag. Moreover, when you have a number of CSV files with a known file encoding, you can use this option to speed up the code generation process.
.RE
.PP
\-d, \-\-delimiters
.RS 4
Delimiter characters to consider for the two dialects, as a single string (default: ',;').
.RE
.PP
\-\-min\-support
.RS 4
Minimum number of records that must clearly fit each dialect (default: 3).
.RE
.PP
\-\-tolerance
.RS 4
Maximum fraction of records on either side of the boundary that may fail to fit the dialect of that side, for instance rows missing a column (default: 0.1).
.RE
.PP
\-j, \-\-json
.RS 4
Print the segments to standard output as a JSON array. Each object has 'start_row', 'end_row', 'start_offset', 'end_offset', and a 'dialect' object with the 'delimiter', 'quotechar', 'escapechar', and 'strict' keys.
.RE
.PP
<path>
.RS 4
Path to the CSV file
.RE
.PP
.sp
